# Persistence

The table separates state into two independent tiers:

- **Transient** — short-lived per-view state: page, search input, sort key/direction, additional sort tiebreakers, currently-open modal row.
- **Persistent** — longer-lived UI preferences: items-per-page, column visibility, column widths, column order, density.

Each tier wires to its own backend. The defaults (`transient: 'snapshot'`, `persistent: 'none'`) preserve pre-persistence behaviour: nothing touches `localStorage` without explicit opt-in.

```typescript
const config: DataTableConfig<UserData> = {
    // ...
    persistence: {
        transient: 'url',
        persistent: 'localStorage',
        urlPrefix: 'dt',
        storageNamespace: config.type,
        urlDebounceMs: 300
    }
};
```

## Options

| Option            | Type                                     | Description                                                                                              | Default                |
|:------------------|:-----------------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------|
| `transient`       | `'snapshot' \| 'url' \| 'none'`          | Backend for page/search/sort/open state.                                                                  | `'snapshot'`           |
| `persistent`      | `'localStorage' \| 'sessionStorage' \| 'none'` | Backend for column prefs and items-per-page.                                                       | `'none'`               |
| `urlPrefix`       | `string`                                 | URL search-param prefix when `transient: 'url'`. Override when multiple tables share a route.            | `'dt'`                 |
| `storageNamespace`| `string`                                 | Storage-key prefix when `persistent: 'localStorage' \| 'sessionStorage'`.                                | `config.type`          |
| `urlDebounceMs`   | `number`                                 | Debounce for URL writes. Bursts of state changes collapse into one `replaceState`.                       | `300`                  |

## Transient backends

- **`'snapshot'`** — wires `capture()` / `restore()` so SvelteKit's [`Snapshot` API](https://kit.svelte.dev/docs/snapshots) restores state on back-navigation. Survives within the SPA session; lost on full reload.
- **`'url'`** — writes to `?dt-page=...&dt-q=...&dt-sortCol=...` via `replaceState`. Shareable, refresh-survivable, back/forward-aware.
- **`'none'`** — in-memory only. Useful for embedded tables where state should reset each mount.

The transient field keys are stable (`page`, `q`, `sortCol`, `sortDir`, `sortMore`, `open`). They're part of the URL wire format — changing the prefix is the only way to disambiguate, never patch the keys themselves.

## Persistent backends

- **`'localStorage'`** — survives reload and tab close. Keys take the form `${storageNamespace}-${field}` (e.g. `userData-columnWidths`).
- **`'sessionStorage'`** — survives reload, lost on tab close.
- **`'none'`** — in-memory only. The user's settings survive component remounts but reset on reload.

`storageNamespace` defaults to `config.type`, which is already unique per table in your app, so collisions are avoided by default.

## SvelteKit snapshot wiring

`'snapshot'` is the default `transient` backend, but SvelteKit only round-trips it when the page exports a `snapshot`. Wire `capture` / `restore` on the route:

```svelte
<script lang='ts'>
    import type { DataTableState } from 'svelte-advanced-datatable';
    import type { Snapshot } from './$types.js';

    let dataTable = $state<DataTable>();

    export const snapshot: Snapshot<DataTableState | undefined> = {
        capture: () => dataTable?.capture(),
        restore: (s) => dataTable?.restore(s)
    };
</script>

<DataTable bind:this={dataTable} {config} {dataSource} />
```

Or pass `initialState` + `captureState` directly to drive state from outside SvelteKit (e.g. saving snapshots to your own store):

```svelte
<DataTable
    {config}
    {dataSource}
    initialState={savedState}
    captureState={(s) => persistToBackend(s)}
/>
```

## Multiple tables on one route

Two tables sharing `transient: 'url'` will fight over the same query params unless you give them different prefixes. Same story for the persistent storage namespace.

```typescript
const usersConfig    = { type: 'users',    persistence: { transient: 'url', urlPrefix: 'u' } };
const projectsConfig = { type: 'projects', persistence: { transient: 'url', urlPrefix: 'p' } };
```

The library detects collisions at mount and logs a console warning when two tables share the same URL prefix or storage namespace.

